          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Nineveh conventionally signifies a wicked Gentile city whose people repent, receive divine mercy, and stand as witnesses against the unrepentant; it also has a complementary judgment-on-empire motif.\",\"book_summary\":\"Nineveh signifies repentant Gentile peoples who rise in judgment against the unrepentant, described as a great city of the sea.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Repentant Gentiles who exemplify repentance and condemn unrepentant hearers.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Gentile peoples who repent and rise in judgment.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"No material boundary disagreement. The book selects the standard Matthew/Jonah repentance motif without discussing Nineveh's additional role as an oppressive empire under judgment; omission of that complementary motif does not amount to its rejection.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None.\",\"rationale\":\"The book's core identification closely matches the consensus's primary Jonah–Matthew reading: Nineveh represents repentant Gentiles whose response condemns the unrepentant. The phrase “great city of the sea” adds imagery but does not replace or materially alter that central referent.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Matthew 12:41 directly supports repentance and rising in judgment, but the printed citation alone does not establish “city of the sea” and only indirectly supports extending the historical men of Nineveh into a general symbol of Gentile peoples.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
